WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.340
Drip.

00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:02.340
Drip.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.340
Drip.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:08.550
If you see the telltale black mucus dripping
from the wall behind you, splattering down

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:12.080
from the ceiling, then it is already too late
for you.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:18.530
The plaster will start to split, the wood
will warp and rot, metal will rust.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:24.449
You should try to run, attempt to hide, but
know that he is coming for you.

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:32.130
The old man, limping along relentlessly, never
stopping to rest, to eat, to catch his breath.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.680
He has no need for such human things anymore.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:41.149
You can’t fight him, you can’t reason
with him, and you can’t kill what might

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:44.059
as well already be dead.

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:48.129
Just when you think you’ve found a safe
place to hide, you’ll hear a creaking from

00:00:48.129 --> 00:00:49.670
the floor below you.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:54.649
A blinding pain as something sharp slashes
through your Achilles tendon is the last thing

00:00:54.649 --> 00:01:01.430
you will feel before the decrepit nightmare
vision drags you to his lair, never to return.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:07.540
Even if you could escape now, the sickness
is already in you, skin already slackening

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:10.189
and muscles beginning to liquify.

00:01:10.189 --> 00:01:19.159
He is rot, he is death, he is the disintegration
of all hope and light, and he has made you

00:01:19.159 --> 00:01:20.159
his prey.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:24.009
We’ve covered SCP-106, or The Old Man, before.

00:01:24.009 --> 00:01:28.969
We’ve talked about his abilities, his hunting
patterns, an incident in which he escaped,

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:31.969
and a variety of theories regarding his origin.

00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:35.049
It is unknown for sure where exactly the Old
Man came from.

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:39.979
There are three major theories as to his true
origin, which created one of the most fearsome

00:01:39.979 --> 00:01:43.469
anomalies the SCP Foundation has ever come
across.

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:48.159
Perhaps he was a deeply disturbed World War
I soldier who gazed too long into something

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:49.929
better left untouched.

00:01:49.929 --> 00:01:55.119
Perhaps he was never human at all, but instead
a divergent humanoid species that preyed on

00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:57.079
early, prehistoric mankind.

00:01:57.079 --> 00:02:04.139
Today, we’re focusing on the third possibility,
and arguably the most tragic of the three.

00:02:04.139 --> 00:02:09.939
It begins with another man we’ve covered
on the channel before, named Dr. Robert Scranton,

00:02:09.939 --> 00:02:12.580
and his wife, Dr. Anna Lang.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:17.190
You probably know them for two things: The
Scranton Reality Anchor, and the accidental

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:21.790
discovery of SCP-3001, or the Red Reality.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:25.599
In case you haven’t seen our video on the
subject, here’s a brief recap of the whole

00:02:25.599 --> 00:02:27.370
unfortunate affair.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:33.340
On January 2, 2000, at Site 120, Robert and
Anna were hard at work as the Head Researchers

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.790
developing an experimental device known as
the “Lang-Scranton Stabilizer”.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:42.959
The LSS was intended to work as a Reality
Anchor, but it was in its early stages and

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:44.219
still unstable.

00:02:44.219 --> 00:02:49.709
A seismic event rocked the research site,
damaging several LSS units, and creating a

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:54.500
wormhole that transported Dr. Scranton to
the pocket dimension hereafter referred to

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.159
as SCP-3001.

00:02:57.159 --> 00:03:04.170
This little pocket of space, this impossible
world just adjacent to ours, has some unusual

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.170
properties.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:10.180
It causes matter within it to deteriorate
as its Hume Level approaches that of the environment

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:14.189
- Humes being the measurement unit for reality
stability.

00:03:14.189 --> 00:03:20.599
Robert Scranton was trapped there in the Red
Reality, alive, for a terrifyingly long time.

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:25.040
Five years, eleven months, and twenty-one
days before the logs he kept ended.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.840
There, he struggled to hold on to himself.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:33.860
He grasped desperately at shreds of his humanity,
his memories, even as the world around him

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:35.639
slowly ate them away.

00:03:35.639 --> 00:03:39.400
Finally, only one thing remained: Anna.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:40.400
His wife.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.329
He could just barely remember her name, remember
that he wanted to get back to her.

00:03:45.329 --> 00:03:51.319
There was a brief moment of hope, on December
23, 2005, when the LSS Control Panel reappeared

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:53.109
in a testing lab at Site-120.

00:03:53.109 --> 00:03:56.340
Dr. Anna Lang arrived to monitor the situation.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:02.870
A mass of bloody organic matter appeared alongside
it, and as Anna called out for her lost love,

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:07.750
something clattered to the floor and broke
her heart: her husband’s wedding ring, stained

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:13.700
with unidentified organic matter, smelling
of black blood and spoiled meat.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:18.440
She collapsed under the weight of the possibility
that her husband was no longer lost in a physical

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.229
sense, but in another sense entirely.

00:04:21.229 --> 00:04:27.990
That this mass of flesh, unrecognizable as
human, might be the man she once loved, tying

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:33.270
his favorite tie for him on date nights, dancing
drunkenly to Living on a Prayer, collapsing

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:37.780
on the couch together breathless with laughter
and the sheer joy of one another.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:42.160
That he could be both here, at last, but gone
forever.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.919
Dr. Anna Lang was taken to the medical bay
and treated for shock.

00:04:45.919 --> 00:04:49.090
Years passed, after Anna’s last encounter
with her husband.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:53.240
She carried the ring with her in her pocket
every day for the first few.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:58.080
After five years, she threaded a chain through
it, wearing it around her neck under her clothes.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.639
After ten more years, she stopped noticing
it was even on anymore.

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:06.900
It had become a part of her, engraved on her
heart with the memories of the man she had

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:09.520
loved, lost, and grieved.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.139
With nothing to go home to at the end of the
day, no one to share her time off with, Anna

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:17.150
began to pour herself deeper into her work.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.840
She would never remarry, she knew that.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.070
Robert had been the only man for her.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:27.030
Now, there was only her commitment to the
Foundation and the research she did there,

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:31.160
and the silent promise she made to herself
that she would never let anything like the

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.540
events of January 2, 2000, happen again.

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:35.650
But will they?

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:39.819
With no reason to go home, Anna’s nights
at the lab grew later and later.

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:43.340
Her colleagues often joked that she might
as well sleep there, since that was the only

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.560
thing she went home to do.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:49.800
The sadness behind her eyes tended to cut
their jokes short soon after making them,

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:50.800
though.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:51.960
Anna liked the late-night work.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.650
The lab was still, almost serene.

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:59.360
She could disappear into the research, the
calculations, let them take over and quiet

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.000
the noise in her mind.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:05.200
The lights were dimmed, no one was asking
her inane questions or bustling around while

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:07.270
she tried to focus.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:12.889
There was nothing but her notes and textbooks,
the light of her lone desk lamp glowing softly

00:06:12.889 --> 00:06:14.819
in the dark, and the work.

00:06:14.819 --> 00:06:19.190
It was on one of these late nights, buried
in a mountain of paperwork, that Anna began

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:21.530
to hear a strange sound.

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:25.569
She didn’t notice it at first, absorbed
as she was in the sea of calculations.

00:06:25.569 --> 00:06:30.440
But soon it began to permeate the room, breaking
through her focus.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:36.530
A rustling sound, a crackling of sorts, quietly
murmuring into her ears.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:40.740
Though she was vaguely aware of it, the way
you might be aware of the wind rustling through

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:46.039
the trees outside your bedroom window, she
didn’t truly hear it until she smelled something

00:06:46.039 --> 00:06:47.840
faint but foul.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:52.770
It was, no matter how muted, the unmistakable
scent of rot.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:56.950
She stepped down from her lab stool, her joints
creaking with effort as she worked out the

00:06:56.950 --> 00:06:58.629
stiffness in her muscles.

00:06:58.629 --> 00:07:03.169
She adjusted her glasses, peering into the
shadows for the source of the sound, but saw

00:07:03.169 --> 00:07:04.560
nothing of note.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.600
She took a step forward into the dark, then
another, then another.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.780
Behind her, the desk lamp flickered, then
cut out entirely.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:16.320
She stopped, blinking in the sudden complete
darkness, and sudden silence.

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.620
The noise had stopped.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:22.220
The only sound was her breathing, quickened
with fear.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:26.320
She pulled the phone from her pocket and turned
on its flashlight, using its weak beam to

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:27.540
light her way.

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:31.479
She turned back to her desk, looking for the
issue that caused the light to cut out.

00:07:31.479 --> 00:07:35.340
Perhaps a short in the wire, or a bulb that
needed changing.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:39.259
Before she could take a look at the lamp,
however, something else caught her eye.

00:07:39.259 --> 00:07:44.750
There was something slick and red on her stack
of papers, dripping onto the clean white surface

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.410
and staining it with gore.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:53.830
A closer look revealed what it was, and a
shiver ran down her spine: a kidney, fresh

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:54.900
and human.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.129
She couldn’t bring herself to touch it,
but somehow knew that if she did, it would

00:07:59.129 --> 00:08:00.259
be warm.

00:08:00.259 --> 00:08:05.180
Her heart pounded in her suddenly tightening
chest as the noise resumed behind her, wet,

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:07.510
slapping, louder than before.

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:11.310
Her feet moved of their own accord as she
walked toward the sound, holding the flashlight

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:13.610
in front of her in a shaking hand.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:17.430
The black wall of the lab, illuminated by
the light, was smeared with something dark

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:21.840
and strange, slick like oil but with a rotten
smell.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.780
A closer look revealed that the wall itself
was beginning to change, drooping limply like

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:28.180
wet cardboard.

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:32.500
She froze, watching helplessly as the wall
bubbled, crumbling to pieces.

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:37.690
Suddenly, a hand emerged from the wall, grey
skin slick with black fluid.

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:40.920
Its fingers, surprisingly strong, gripped
her arm.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:45.350
She tore it away, scrambling back, and saw
that pieces of her lab coat had come off,

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:48.040
melting into the grey skin of the alien hand.

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:52.200
As the arm reached for her again, she stumbled
and dropped her phone onto the floor, casting

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:56.610
its light against the wall and the figure
worming its way out.

00:08:56.610 --> 00:09:03.650
There it was, an old man, naked and grey with
slimy, decaying skin coming off in pieces.

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:08.620
Its throat was shredded, an open wound, and
it grinned at her with stiff shriveled lips

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.890
stretched over blackened teeth.

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:15.030
Its eyes were the worst part, even amidst
the rest of the walking corpse.

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:19.410
They were almost like a man’s, they were
correct in shape and placement, but they were

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:20.410
wrong.

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:24.250
They were empty, clouded over, and void of
any warmth or mercy.

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:28.940
As he stood there, the ground beneath him
began to dissipate, melting into an oily puddle

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:32.210
that enveloped her phone and snuffed out its
light.

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:36.640
There was only one thing left for Anna to
do, and so…she ran.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.630
She turned and made a break for the lab doors,
lungs burning from the effort.

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:45.100
She cried out to the guard on night duty,
“Frank!” her voice was shrill and pleading.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:49.630
He appeared from around the corner, gun already
drawn, eyes wide and alert at the panic he

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:51.440
must have heard in her voice.

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.800
As Frank tried to calm her down, and she tried
desperately to explain what she had seen,

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:59.620
the old man forced his way through the lab
doors, leaving a trail of corrosive black

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:01.170
slime in his wake.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:04.740
Frank fired his gun, hitting the old man in
the chest twice.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:09.950
He collapsed, sinking into the floor as his
excretion broke away the tiles beneath him.

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:15.130
As Anna and Frank debated what to do next,
a drip of black mucus landed on Frank’s

00:10:15.130 --> 00:10:16.860
radio from above.

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:19.810
Another drop fell, landing directly in his
eye.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:23.890
He screamed in agony, the mucus dissolving
his eyeball on contact.

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:29.260
Next, the empty grin appeared through the
ceiling, and the old man fell on top of Frank,

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:31.540
tearing out his throat with its bare hands.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:36.120
Anna knew that she had to escape, but where
could she go?

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:37.330
She couldn’t think.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:38.480
Couldn’t plan.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.140
She just knew she had to run.

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:43.780
She ran until her feet suddenly stopped obeying.

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:44.780
She was stuck.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:48.220
She was sinking into the ground, and then…she
was falling.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:55.390
Down, down into a moldy, dusty hallway, dark
except for a nauseating greenish-grey glow.

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:58.890
She had been pulled into some new, nightmarish
world.

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:03.690
She ran through a nearby door, and found herself,
shockingly, in her old apartment.

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:04.870
How had she gotten here?

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:07.540
How did the old man know this place?

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:10.400
There was her bed, her desk, her closet.

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:14.410
She flung open the closet door, to find a
pile of rotten corpses that poured out and

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:16.090
slopped onto the floor.

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:19.890
She covered her mouth to choke back a scream,
turned and fled.

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:22.420
Then, she was back in the hall.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:28.630
This time, she recognized it: it was a twisted,
warped version of Site-120.

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:33.320
In front of her, there he was: the old man
had found her again.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:36.070
And now, she was in his territory.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:41.470
He stared at her with those hollow eyes, and
for the first time, spoke.

00:11:41.470 --> 00:11:42.470
“Red.”

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:44.350
He growled.

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:46.990
She stopped in her tracks, frozen to the spot.

00:11:46.990 --> 00:11:52.320
What he said next turned her stomach and brought
tears to her eyes.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:53.320
“Anna.”

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:56.500
He knew her name.

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:00.710
Again, he repeated, “Anna.”

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:05.990
He reached out to her with a hand that had
a tan line, where a wedding ring had once

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:06.990
been.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.270
The same ring burning against her skin under
her shirt.

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:14.330
He cupped her cheek, and she felt the skin
sting and blister.

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:19.250
He kissed her, her teeth melting and fusing,
tongue turning to mush that dribbled down

00:12:19.250 --> 00:12:21.060
her throat and choked her.

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:25.850
Her last thought, through the fear and the
despair, was the ache of love.

00:12:25.850 --> 00:12:28.120
Her husband was not lost.

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:29.370
He was found.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.680
And he had found her too.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.460
Now, they would be together forever.

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:41.640
Now go check out “SCP-106 The Old Man”
and “SCP-106 - The Old Man Origin Theories”

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:45.270
for more on the terrifying story of this iconic
anomaly.

